1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,647
Solids are usually the densest, because their particles are packed tightly.

2
00:00:04,647 --> 00:00:08,439
Liquid particles can move, so liquids are usually less dense.

3
00:00:08,439 --> 00:00:12,230
Gas particles are far apart, so gases have the lowest density.

4
00:00:13,291 --> 00:00:19,590
Structure matters too. Pumice and granite rocks can be the same size, but pumice is full of air holes,

5
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:25,032
making it ten times less dense than granite. Metals are generally denser than non-metals.

6
00:00:26,093 --> 00:00:27,867
Temperature changes density.

7
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:32,759
Heated air expands and becomes less dense, so hot air rises and cold air sinks.

8
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:36,245
That's how hot-air balloons fly and how sea breezes form.

9
00:00:37,306 --> 00:00:43,115
Here are typical densities of six materials at room temperature, in grams per cubic centimetre.

10
00:00:44,176 --> 00:00:49,741
Cork is only 0.24, ice 0.92 and water exactly 1. Aluminium is 2.7, iron 7.9, and lead 11.3.

11
00:00:50,802 --> 00:00:56,061
Pause and decide: which of these materials would float in water, and which would sink?
